// An enum owns its variants' layout, so its own fields are spliced into every
// variant as clones. A variant declaring one of those names again is a duplicate
// rather than an override: the enum put the field there, and nothing in a variant
// replaces it.
//
// This is the one splice left in the language. A trait contributes no fields --
// what its fields state is a requirement the type declares for itself -- so the
// duplicate can only arise here, between an enum and a variant written inside it.
//
// Name-resolution stage: the fields are spliced while the variant is resolved,
// which is before any signature or layout question could be asked. The clone
// keeps the enum's location, so the diagnostic lands on the enum's declaration of
// the field; the frame line that follows it points at the variant that caused it
// and carries no code of its own to annotate.

enum Shape {
  pub common i32;  //~ ErrorDupName:7 "Enum may not splice in a duplicate field name"

  struct Rect {
    pub common i32;
    pub w i32;
  }

  struct Circle {
    pub r i32;
  }
}
